According to ideal gas equation
$\begin{array}{rr} 
& p V=n R T \\
\therefore \quad V & \propto \frac{1}{p}
\end{array}\therefore$ To increase the volume of gas by $10 \%$, the pressure of gas should be decrease by $10 \%$.

According to gas equation,
$\begin{aligned}
& p V &=n R T \\
\therefore & V &=\frac{n R T}{p}
\end{aligned}$
As both $p$ and $T$ are double, $V$ remains constant i.e., $4 \mathrm{dm}^{3}$.

Given, $p_{\text {total }}=25$ bar, $\chi_{O_{2}}=0.18$
Using the relation; $\chi_{\mathrm{O}_{2}}+\chi_{\mathrm{Ne}}=1$
\begin{aligned}
&\chi_{\mathrm{Ne}}=1-0.18=0.82 \\
&p_{\mathrm{Ne}}=\chi_{\mathrm{Ne}} \times p_{\text {tota }}=0.82 \times 25=20.5 \mathrm{bar}
\end{aligned}

Distance travelled by gas is directly proportional to rate of diffusion

\begin{aligned}
&\therefore \quad r_{\mathrm{NH}_{3}}\left(l_{1}\right) \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{M_{\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}} \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{17}} \\
&\quad r_{\mathrm{HCl}}\left(l_{2}\right) \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{M_{\mathrm{HCl}}}} \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{365}} \\
&\because \quad r_{\mathrm{NH}_{3}}>r_{\mathrm{HCl}}
\end{aligned}

$\therefore$ Length travelled by $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is greater than $\mathrm{HCl}$. Hence, white ring of $\mathrm{NH}_{4} \mathrm{Cl}$ is formed nearer to $\mathrm{HCl}$ end.

Kinetic energy $=\frac{3}{2} R T$
\begin{aligned}
&=\frac{3}{2} \times 8.314 \times 300 \\
&=3741.3 \mathrm{~J}=3.74 \mathrm{~kJ}
\end{aligned}

$v_{\mathrm{rms}}=\sqrt{\frac{3 R T}{M}}v_{\mathrm{rms}} \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{M}}$, where $M=$ molecular mass

\begin{aligned}
&\Rightarrow v_{\mathrm{rms}_{\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right)}}: v_{\mathrm{rms}_{(\mathrm{He})}}: v_{\mathrm{rms}_{\left(\mathrm{SO}_{2}\right)}} \\
&\Rightarrow \quad \frac{1}{\sqrt{16}}: \frac{1}{\sqrt{4}}: \frac{1}{\sqrt{64}} \Rightarrow \frac{1}{4}: \frac{1}{2}: \frac{1}{8} \\
&\Rightarrow \quad 2: 4: 1
\end{aligned}
